  <!-- MAIN -->
  <main class="lesson-main">

    <div class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="../../../../../entrance-exam/index.html">Entrance Exam</a> ›
      <a href="../../index.html">Grade 12</a> ›
      <a href="../index.html">Mathematics</a> ›
      Unit 4 · Lesson 3
    </div>

    <div class="lesson-header">
      <div class="lesson-unit-tag">Unit 4 · Integral Calculus · Final Lesson</div>
      <h1>∫ Applications of Integration</h1>
      <p>Put integration to work: compute areas between curves, find distances from velocity functions, and solve the class of real-world problems the Ethiopian entrance exam tests most frequently.</p>
      <div class="lesson-meta">
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">📖 <strong>Reading:</strong> ~20 min</div>
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">✏️ <strong>Examples:</strong> 5 worked</div>
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">🎯 <strong>Quiz:</strong> 5 questions</div>
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">🎥 <strong>Videos:</strong> 4 resources</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════
         SECTION 1: INTRODUCTION
    ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <div class="section-block" id="intro">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">🎯</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Introduction</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">What you will learn in this lesson</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="content-box">
        <p style="font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem">
          In Lessons 1 and 2 you built the mechanics of integration: how to find antiderivatives and how to evaluate definite integrals using F(b) − F(a). In this final lesson of Unit 4 you use those tools to solve real problems.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem">
          The two most important applications tested on the Ethiopian Grade 12 entrance exam are:
        </p>
        <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:1rem;margin-bottom:1rem">
          <div style="background:var(--brand-light);border:1px solid #BBF7D0;border-radius:var(--radius-lg);padding:1.25rem">
            <div style="font-size:1.5rem;margin-bottom:.5rem">📐</div>
            <div style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.35rem">Area Under a Curve</div>
            <div style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--text-muted);line-height:1.6">Use ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx to find the area between a curve and the x-axis.</div>
          </div>
          <div style="background:var(--brand-light);border:1px solid #BBF7D0;border-radius:var(--radius-lg);padding:1.25rem">
            <div style="font-size:1.5rem;margin-bottom:.5rem">🚀</div>
            <div style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.35rem">Area Between Two Curves</div>
            <div style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--text-muted);line-height:1.6">Use ∫ₐᵇ [f(x) − g(x)] dx when the exam gives two functions.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--text)">By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
        <ul style="margin-top:.75rem;padding-left:1.25rem;font-size:.9rem;line-height:2;color:var(--text)">
          <li>Find the area between a curve y = f(x) and the x-axis over [a, b]</li>
          <li>Handle cases where f(x) dips below the x-axis (negative area)</li>
          <li>Find the area enclosed between two curves y = f(x) and y = g(x)</li>
          <li>Identify the limits of integration by finding intersection points</li>
          <li>Interpret integration as total displacement from a velocity function</li>
          <li>Avoid the most common exam traps with signed vs total area</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════
         SECTION 2: EXPLANATION
    ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <div class="section-block" id="explanation">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">📖</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Explanation</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">Complete theory with visual aids</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- 2.1 Area under a curve -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.1 Area Between a Curve and the x-axis</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem">
          When f(x) ≥ 0 on [a, b], the definite integral gives the exact area between the curve and the x-axis directly:
        </p>

        <div class="definition-box">
          <div class="def-label">📌 Area Formula — Curve Above x-axis</div>
          <p>
            If f(x) ≥ 0 for all x in [a, b], then:<br><br>
            <strong style="font-size:1.1rem;font-family:Georgia,serif">Area = ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a)</strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Area diagram: curve above x-axis -->
        <div class="area-diagram">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 520 210" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:520px">
            <!-- axes -->
            <line x1="40" y1="175" x2="480" y2="175" stroke="#CBD5E1" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="60" y1="20" x2="60" y2="188" stroke="#CBD5E1" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <!-- shaded area -->
            <path d="M130,175 Q170,60 220,80 Q270,100 320,40 L320,175 Z" fill="rgba(34,197,94,.18)" stroke="none"/>
            <!-- curve -->
            <path d="M100,150 Q140,30 220,80 Q270,110 320,40 Q360,0 420,80" fill="none" stroke="#14532D" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"/>
            <!-- a and b markers -->
            <line x1="130" y1="170" x2="130" y2="180" stroke="#14532D" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="127" y="193" font-size="12" fill="#14532D" font-weight="700" font-family="Georgia,serif">a</text>
            <line x1="320" y1="170" x2="320" y2="180" stroke="#14532D" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="317" y="193" font-size="12" fill="#14532D" font-weight="700" font-family="Georgia,serif">b</text>
            <!-- area label -->
            <text x="200" y="140" font-size="12" fill="#166534" font-weight="700" font-family="Georgia,serif">Area = ∫ₐᵇ f(x)dx</text>
            <!-- axis labels -->
            <text x="468" y="179" font-size="12" fill="#64748B" font-family="sans-serif">x</text>
            <text x="46" y="18" font-size="12" fill="#64748B" font-family="sans-serif">y</text>
            <text x="415" y="78" font-size="11" fill="#14532D" font-family="sans-serif">y = f(x)</text>
          </svg>
          <p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--text-muted);margin-top:.5rem">When f(x) ≥ 0, the integral equals the shaded area directly.</p>
        </div>

        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem">
          <strong>When f(x) goes below the x-axis</strong>, the integral gives a <em>signed area</em> — regions below count as negative. To find <strong>total area</strong> (always positive), split at the zeros and take the absolute value of each piece:
        </p>

        <div class="definition-box">
          <div class="def-label">📌 Total Area When f(x) Changes Sign</div>
          <p>
            If f(x) changes sign at c ∈ (a, b):<br><br>
            <strong style="font-family:Georgia,serif">Total Area = |∫ₐᶜ f(x) dx| + |∫ᶜᵇ f(x) dx|</strong><br><br>
            Find where f(x) = 0, split the integral there, and take absolute values.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="note-box">
          <div class="note-label">⚠ Key Exam Distinction</div>
          <p><strong>Signed area</strong> (net area): use ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx directly. <strong>Total area</strong> (always positive): split at zeros, use absolute values. The exam question always tells you which to find — read carefully.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- 2.2 Area between two curves -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.2 Area Between Two Curves</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem">
          When two curves are given, the area of the region between them is the integral of their <em>vertical gap</em>: top curve minus bottom curve.
        </p>

        <div class="definition-box">
          <div class="def-label">📌 Area Between Two Curves</div>
          <p>
            If f(x) ≥ g(x) on [a, b] (f is on top, g is on bottom):<br><br>
            <strong style="font-size:1.1rem;font-family:Georgia,serif">Area = ∫ₐᵇ [f(x) − g(x)] dx</strong><br><br>
            The limits a and b are usually the x-coordinates where the curves intersect (set f(x) = g(x) and solve).
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Two curves diagram -->
        <div class="area-diagram">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 520 220" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:520px">
            <!-- axes -->
            <line x1="40" y1="180" x2="480" y2="180" stroke="#CBD5E1" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="60" y1="20" x2="60" y2="193" stroke="#CBD5E1" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <!-- shaded region between curves -->
            <path d="M120,155 Q200,60 320,100 L320,145 Q200,120 120,155 Z" fill="rgba(34,197,94,.18)" stroke="none"/>
            <!-- top curve f(x) -->
            <path d="M80,170 Q160,50 280,80 Q360,100 440,60" fill="none" stroke="#14532D" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"/>
            <!-- bottom curve g(x) -->
            <path d="M80,175 Q160,140 280,130 Q360,125 440,120" fill="none" stroke="#2563EB" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-dasharray="5,3"/>
            <!-- intersection markers -->
            <circle cx="120" cy="155" r="4" fill="#14532D"/>
            <circle cx="320" cy="100" r="4" fill="#14532D"/>
            <!-- a and b markers -->
            <text x="115" y="197" font-size="12" fill="#14532D" font-weight="700" font-family="Georgia,serif">a</text>
            <text x="315" y="197" font-size="12" fill="#14532D" font-weight="700" font-family="Georgia,serif">b</text>
            <!-- labels -->
            <text x="440" y="57" font-size="11" fill="#14532D" font-family="sans-serif">y = f(x) (top)</text>
            <text x="440" y="118" font-size="11" fill="#2563EB" font-family="sans-serif">y = g(x) (bottom)</text>
            <text x="190" y="100" font-size="11" fill="#166534" font-weight="700" font-family="Georgia,serif">∫[f−g]dx</text>
            <text x="460" y="184" font-size="12" fill="#64748B" font-family="sans-serif">x</text>
            <text x="46" y="18" font-size="12" fill="#64748B" font-family="sans-serif">y</text>
          </svg>
          <p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--text-muted);margin-top:.5rem">The shaded area equals ∫ₐᵇ [f(x) − g(x)] dx. Always subtract bottom from top.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="background:var(--surface-2);border-radius:var(--radius-lg);padding:1.25rem;margin-top:.875rem">
          <div style="font-size:.875rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.75rem">3-step process for area between two curves:</div>
          <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(160px,1fr));gap:.875rem">
            <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:.875rem">
              <div style="font-size:.72rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--brand-primary);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.4rem">Step 1</div>
              <div style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--heading)">Find intersections — set f(x) = g(x) and solve for x. These are a and b.</div>
            </div>
            <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:.875rem">
              <div style="font-size:.72rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--brand-primary);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.4rem">Step 2</div>
              <div style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--heading)">Identify which curve is on top: f(x) ≥ g(x) on [a, b].</div>
            </div>
            <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:.875rem">
              <div style="font-size:.72rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--brand-primary);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.4rem">Step 3</div>
              <div style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--heading)">Evaluate ∫ₐᵇ [f(x) − g(x)] dx using the FTC.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- 2.3 Displacement and distance -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.3 Displacement from a Velocity Function</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem">
          If v(t) is the velocity of an object at time t, then integrating v(t) gives displacement (change in position):
        </p>

        <div class="definition-box">
          <div class="def-label">📌 Displacement and Distance</div>
          <p>
            <strong>Displacement</strong> (signed, may cancel):<br>
            <span style="font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--brand-primary)">Δx = ∫ₜ₁ᵗ² v(t) dt</span><br><br>
            <strong>Total distance</strong> (always positive):<br>
            <span style="font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--brand-primary)">Distance = ∫ₜ₁ᵗ² |v(t)| dt</span><br><br>
            Split the integral where v(t) = 0 and take absolute values of each piece.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="note-box">
          <div class="note-label">📝 Displacement vs Distance</div>
          <p>Displacement can be zero even if the object moved — for example, if it went 5 m forward then 5 m back. Total distance counts all motion as positive. The exam will specify which one it wants.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════
         SECTION 3: FORMULA REFERENCE
    ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <div class="section-block" id="formula">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">📋</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Formula Reference Card</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">Key formulas — memorize these for the exam</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="formula-card">
        <div class="formula-card-title">📐 Lesson 3 — Applications of Integration</div>
        <div class="formula-grid">
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Area Under Curve</div>
            <div class="formula-math">A = ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx</div>
            <div class="formula-note">When f(x) ≥ 0 on [a, b]. Uses FTC: F(b) − F(a).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Total Area (sign changes)</div>
            <div class="formula-math">A = |∫ₐᶜ f dx| + |∫ᶜᵇ f dx|</div>
            <div class="formula-note">Split at zeros of f, take absolute values</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Area Between Curves</div>
            <div class="formula-math">A = ∫ₐᵇ [f(x)−g(x)] dx</div>
            <div class="formula-note">f is top curve, g is bottom. Find a, b from f(x)=g(x).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Displacement</div>
            <div class="formula-math">Δx = ∫ₜ₁ᵗ² v(t) dt</div>
            <div class="formula-note">Signed — forward and backward cancel</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Total Distance</div>
            <div class="formula-math">D = ∫ₜ₁ᵗ² |v(t)| dt</div>
            <div class="formula-note">Split where v(t)=0, sum absolute values</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Find Limits of Integration</div>
            <div class="formula-math">Set f(x) = g(x), solve for x</div>
            <div class="formula-note">The solutions are your limits a and b</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════
         SECTION 4: WORKED EXAMPLES
    ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <div class="section-block" id="examples">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">✏️</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Worked Examples</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">5 exam-style problems solved step by step</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Example 1 -->
      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header">
          <span class="example-num">Example 1</span>
          <span class="example-title">Area Between a Curve and the x-axis</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem">
            <strong>Problem:</strong> Find the area enclosed by y = x² and the x-axis between x = 0 and x = 3.
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 1</span>
            <div class="step-text">Check that f(x) = x² ≥ 0 on [0, 3]. Yes — it is always non-negative. So we can use the integral directly.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 2</span>
            <div class="step-text">Find the antiderivative:<br>
              <span class="math">F(x) = x³/3</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 3</span>
            <div class="step-text">Apply the FTC:<br>
              <span class="math">[x³/3]₀³ = 3³/3 − 0³/3 = 27/3 − 0 = 9</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ Area = <strong>9 square units</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Example 2 -->
      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header">
          <span class="example-num">Example 2</span>
          <span class="example-title">Total Area When Curve Crosses x-axis</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem">
            <strong>Problem:</strong> Find the <em>total area</em> bounded by y = x² − 4 and the x-axis between x = −2 and x = 2.
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 1</span>
            <div class="step-text">Find where f(x) = x² − 4 = 0:<br>
              <span class="math">x² = 4 → x = ±2</span><br>
              So f(x) = 0 at x = −2 and x = 2 (the endpoints). On (−2, 2), x² − 4 &lt; 0, so the curve is below the x-axis.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 2</span>
            <div class="step-text">The curve is entirely below the x-axis on (−2, 2). For total area, take the absolute value of the integral:<br>
              <span class="math">Area = |∫₋₂² (x² − 4) dx|</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 3</span>
            <div class="step-text">Evaluate: F(x) = x³/3 − 4x<br>
              <span class="math">F(2) = 8/3 − 8 = 8/3 − 24/3 = −16/3</span><br>
              <span class="math">F(−2) = −8/3 + 8 = −8/3 + 24/3 = 16/3</span><br>
              <span class="math">Integral = −16/3 − 16/3 = −32/3</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 4</span>
            <div class="step-text">Total area = |−32/3| = <span class="math">32/3</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ Total Area = <strong>32/3 square units</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Example 3 -->
      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header">
          <span class="example-num">Example 3</span>
          <span class="example-title">Area Between Two Curves</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem">
            <strong>Problem:</strong> Find the area enclosed between f(x) = x + 2 and g(x) = x².
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 1</span>
            <div class="step-text">Find the intersection points by setting f(x) = g(x):<br>
              <span class="math">x + 2 = x² → x² − x − 2 = 0 → (x−2)(x+1) = 0</span><br>
              So x = −1 and x = 2. These are the limits: a = −1, b = 2.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 2</span>
            <div class="step-text">Check which is on top on (−1, 2). At x = 0: f(0) = 2, g(0) = 0. So f(x) = x + 2 is on top.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 3</span>
            <div class="step-text">Set up and evaluate the integral:<br>
              <span class="math">∫₋₁² [(x+2) − x²] dx = ∫₋₁² (x + 2 − x²) dx</span><br>
              <span class="math">F(x) = x²/2 + 2x − x³/3</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 4</span>
            <div class="step-text">Evaluate F(2) and F(−1):<br>
              <span class="math">F(2) = 4/2 + 4 − 8/3 = 2 + 4 − 8/3 = 6 − 8/3 = 10/3</span><br>
              <span class="math">F(−1) = 1/2 − 2 + 1/3 = 3/6 − 12/6 + 2/6 = −7/6</span><br>
              <span class="math">Area = 10/3 − (−7/6) = 20/6 + 7/6 = 27/6 = 9/2</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ Area = <strong>9/2 square units</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Example 4 -->
      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header">
          <span class="example-num">Example 4</span>
          <span class="example-title">Displacement from a Velocity Function</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem">
            <strong>Problem:</strong> A particle moves with velocity v(t) = 3t² − 6t. Find the displacement and total distance from t = 0 to t = 3.
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 1</span>
            <div class="step-text">Displacement = ∫₀³ (3t² − 6t) dt<br>
              F(t) = t³ − 3t²<br>
              <span class="math">F(3) − F(0) = (27 − 27) − 0 = 0</span><br>
              Displacement = 0 (it returned to its start).
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 2</span>
            <div class="step-text">For total distance, find where v(t) = 0:<br>
              <span class="math">3t² − 6t = 3t(t − 2) = 0 → t = 0 or t = 2</span><br>
              Split at t = 2.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 3</span>
            <div class="step-text">
              Check sign: at t = 1, v(1) = 3 − 6 = −3 &lt; 0. So on (0, 2) the particle moves backward.<br>
              At t = 2.5, v = 3(6.25) − 15 = 3.75 &gt; 0. On (2, 3) it moves forward.<br><br>
              <span class="math">|∫₀² (3t²−6t) dt| = |[t³−3t²]₀²| = |(8−12)−0| = |−4| = 4</span><br>
              <span class="math">∫₂³ (3t²−6t) dt = [t³−3t²]₂³ = (27−27)−(8−12) = 0−(−4) = 4</span><br>
              Total distance = 4 + 4 = 8
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ Displacement = <strong>0</strong> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Total Distance = <strong>8 units</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Example 5 -->
      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header">
          <span class="example-num">Example 5</span>
          <span class="example-title">Area Between a Parabola and a Line</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem">
            <strong>Problem:</strong> Find the area enclosed between y = 4 − x² and y = 0 (the x-axis).
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 1</span>
            <div class="step-text">Find where f(x) = 4 − x² = 0:<br>
              <span class="math">x² = 4 → x = ±2</span><br>
              Limits: a = −2, b = 2.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 2</span>
            <div class="step-text">Check sign on (−2, 2): at x = 0, f(0) = 4 &gt; 0. Curve is above x-axis, so we integrate directly.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Step 3</span>
            <div class="step-text">Evaluate:<br>
              <span class="math">∫₋₂² (4 − x²) dx</span><br>
              F(x) = 4x − x³/3<br>
              <span class="math">F(2) = 8 − 8/3 = 16/3</span><br>
              <span class="math">F(−2) = −8 + 8/3 = −16/3</span><br>
              <span class="math">Area = 16/3 − (−16/3) = 32/3</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ Area = <strong>32/3 square units</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

